A||N||7||Return of Fiero||making an 84 2.5 more reliable||||5||
Z||000000||Return of Fiero||11-13-2001||03:43 PM||||what parts can be upraded, or added to make the engine more reliable. im not looking for fast, just reliable. please post one things that will give me this effect. hp is not the motive, but if it comes with the part, bonus. any way, this is for a daily driver, its a 84 2M4, and my concerns are reliable and economy. thanks for the help.<BR>Myke||||reg||5
Z||000001||Rodrv6||11-13-2001||03:53 PM||||In 330,000 miles of driving my 84 the only trouble I ever had with the engine (other than routine stuff like water pumps and alternators) was the cam timing gear shedding teeth.  It seemed to happen about every 60,000 miles or so.  I put a set of all steel gears in and probably solved the problem for good.  I changed the oil and filter every 5,000 miles.  When I tore it down for a rebuild at 260,000 miles I found a clean engine with little wear.  Even the cylinders still had the original cross-hatch showing and measured within specs.  This was not a "babied" engine at all---I enjoy spirited driving  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I certainly got my money's worth out of it.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroFuker||11-13-2001||03:56 PM||||Install a fire extinguisher<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/tjstrato/redfiero1.jpg<BR>"> <BR>crux.baker.edu/tdonal02||||reg||1
Z||000003||erikred||11-13-2001||04:40 PM||||I agree about the timming gears. The factory fiber ones are crap. I would not waste my time rebuilding a Duke without putting some metal ones in.<P>Don't forget to change your oil VERY OFTEN! It's probably the number 1 way to keep an engine in good condition.<P>Erik-||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pilrulz||11-13-2001||05:37 PM||||a few years ago, my dad but a rebuilt duke in our chevy celebrity station wagon.  we had just started on our first drive around the block when all of a sudden we knew there was a really bad problem.  in less than a half mile, it had shredded the timing gears.  He was mad.  but at least they gave us another engine free.  With the exception of bad heads, that is the worst problem with these engines.  Every once in a while you will find a good engine.  however, this is rare.  basically, to make these engines more reliable, you hang a rabbit's foot from the rear view mirror and cross your fingers.<P>pil||||reg||1
Z||000005||Return of Fiero||11-13-2001||07:08 PM||||other than the 3rd coment, it seems generaly the timing gears are the only true flaw. as far as the fires in the car goes, from what i have seem, it only happenes to the abused and neglected ones. i have never heard of any person having a problem with fires, unless they only opened the trunk to put groceries in. also, it wont make the car more reliable to put a fire extinguisher in the car. there for i dont see how it is relevent to my question.in any event, the timing gears are the only true problem with the duke run under "normal" driving conditions?<P>Myke||||reg||14
Z||000006||Mach10||11-13-2001||07:14 PM||||Wrap the block in duct-tape to hold it together  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously, though, the motors are fine.<P>1) FREQUENT OIL CHANGES... Cheapest damn way to keep any motor running FOREVER. Literally, if done regular from day 1, it's like a rebuild on an installment plan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>2) Frequent tune ups. Keep it running smooth.<P>3) Don't abuse it. The Duke will last forever if it isn't beaten on. Face the fact that it ISN'T DESIGNED as a high-output motor, so don't treat it like one. It doesn't like to be wound up. You can have PLENTY of fun without trying to do burnouts, or showing off all the time. Treat it nice, it'll treat you FINE  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> ||||reg||10
Z||000007||ITB 2M4||11-13-2001||10:45 PM||||I can't emphasize enough about the oil.  The early 2.5L's came from the factory with 3.5 qts. of oil.  How many cars do you know of that are 1 qt. low?  2.5 qts of oil in a mid-engine car is just asking for trouble.  I'm racing an '84 2M4 SE, and I have a 3 qt. Accusump, an oil cooler, and a remote filter, bringing the grand total to 10qts. of oil.  Plus, I have trap doors in the oil pan so that the pick-up doesn't starve for oil.  I'm running the alloy timing gear on the cam as well.  I've run two race weekends so far with this motor, and it routinely touches 5500rpm, without missing a beat so far.  It's also been balanced and blueprinted, which helps with the higher rpm's.  Make sure the cooling system is in good shape as well, and you should have plenty of trouble free miles.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
